{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## WITHDRAWN — no livelock, no lost writes. I misread a proxy metric.\n\nThe claim was that ticket events were not reaching `sync.remote`. **They were.** Direct check —\nare tickets I created minutes before filing this present on the remote?\n\n```\na197-d055 (this very ticket)          :  3 commits on origin/tickets\naac0-dbdf (just-preglacial-yeti)      : 36 commits on origin/tickets, including its close\n1e48-ff69 (evileyed-depletive-flycatcher): 8 commits on origin/tickets\norigin/tickets tip                    : current, seconds old\n```\n\nEvery write landed. The push path was healthy the entire time.\n\n### What I actually measured, and why it proved nothing\n\nI used `git rev-list --count origin/tickets..HEAD` as a stand-in for 'unpushed work' and watched it\ngrow (447 -> 463 -> 532). In a store that fetches, merges and re-pushes, that count includes local\nmerge topology not reachable from the remote ref **even when all of the content has already\nlanded**. A rising number under concurrent write load is ordinary, not a symptom.\n\nI never ran the one check that would have settled it: is the content on the remote. It took a\nsingle command and refutes the entire ticket.\n\n### The errors, stated plainly\n\n1. **I inferred a system failure from a proxy without validating the proxy.** The metric did not\n   mean what I assumed.\n2. **I concluded 'not draining' from a 90-second sample** under heavy write load, which cannot\n   distinguish a queue from a livelock.\n3. **I ran raw git in the tracker** (`fetch`, `push --dry-run`) to investigate, which AGENTS.md\n   bans precisely because ad-hoc git there is unsafe and misleading. The supported door,\n   `rebar tracker-maintenance --status`, reported the store **healthy** — zero stranded entries,\n   zero foreign paths — and I treated that as insufficient rather than as the answer.\n4. **I escalated to the operator and proposed pausing five agents** they had just commissioned,\n   on this evidence.\n\n`rebar doctor` (write locks free) and `tracker-maintenance --status` (store clean) both said\nnothing was wrong. Two supported diagnostics agreed, and I trusted a hand-rolled count over both.\n\n### Nothing survives\n\n- The livelock: **disproven**.\n- Backoff/jitter/batching proposals: solving a problem that does not exist.\n- The operator-warning proposal: there is nothing to warn about; a rising local commit count is\n  normal.\n- The `nuclear-calm-heron` link: that ticket is real on its own evidence, but nothing here\n  corroborates it. Removing the association rather than leaving a false one attached.\n\nClosing `not_a_bug`. If someone later observes ticket CONTENT genuinely absent from\n`origin/tickets` — checked by searching the remote for the commits, not by counting local\ntopology — that is a real finding and deserves its own ticket with that evidence."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787293573717931001,"uuid":"f998795a-5e52-44ac-a762-2325446fa5b8"}